rules to try to break down sort of if we  think of data as the new oil so to speak  or can we apply those older mechanisms  those traditional legal frameworks that  we have in order to manage it  differently  and this is actually where in a way the  regulators fail because they're  consistently sort of falling back to uh  potentially either non-innovative or not  bold enough reforms in order to truly  create a different playing field because  i fully agree with what was said in a  sense it's not about uh questions of  content moderation or specific rights or  do we have enough international  instruments at stake rather what's the  main problem is the lack of  understanding that we have right now  globally  such a mixture between public and  private power in the sense that we're  lacking the kind of innovativeness of  new accountability instruments and new  accountability thinking in order to  match the new realities in which we're  facing with and then following falling  back down to questions the first  amendment speech regulation or issues  around constitutional rights and privacy  etc those are very traditional  frameworks which are useful and of  course to be respected however i do  think right now  we need new efforts and in this regard i  think a lot of colleagues that i'm  seeing across the board are gathering  data there is a different kind of  momentum which is much more promising  that we're seeing in the sense of  understanding the nature of the bees  better in order to see actually how do  we really deal with regulation because  there's the lack of fuel regulation is  not good bad regulation is equally not  okay even if there's lack of compliance  because we simply then even further  discourage the kind of and so that has  been a little bit the challenge finding  that nuance finding that right tone with  which to regulate this  and considering that it's not a national  issue considering that we're truly  talking about as you said global  corporations which are based in one  country operate in another have their  data sets stored in another place  they're full form the internet and and  entire areas of countries let's say  myanmar that you mentioned before for a  lot of people facebook is synonymous the  equivalent to the internet in full so  there's a lot of also discrepancies  across the globe in the kind of the  playing field as i mentioned that we're  seeing with between regulators private  actors and what does that mean for  citizens and for the legitimate  interests and values that we want to  protect uh quinn mchugh is there a a  collusion between the governments and  social media companies that actually  stops any kind of real  regulation from coming into place after  all if they are corporations then they  get tax benefits they get freedom to do  whatever they want to do to make a  profit and they don't have to have the  same laws that say public broadcasters  uh do or other  media companies do so is there an  advantage for this vagueness for the  social media companies and for the  governments  i think one of the things that we all  need to remember as you pointed out is  that at the end of the day these are  profit-making entities who are private  corporations private or publicly traded  corporations who are driven by the  bottom line  and as much as they attempt to protect  freedom of expression or talk about  freedom of expression at the end of the  day they need to do what they need to do  from their own corporate perspective to  protect their company to protect their  own staff and to protect the profits  that they make um we just mentioned  about how a number of countries  the distinction between the internet and  facebook doesn't exist because the only  internet is the facebook internet that  people are given access to  that raises questions of as you pointed  out are they do they need to be treated  the same way that other corporations do  because  corporations have their their own  interests and some of the things that  we've seen in particular uh you  mentioned at the beginning of this uh  segment how india has threatened some of  twitter's staff with the rest for  non-compliance with certain indian  regulations or laws  we are increasingly seeing that  governments have absolutely no  compunction to to basically hold staff  hostage to require companies to hold  offices to hold staff in certain  locations we see that with russia with  their landing law um that came into  effect this year we've seen it in turkey  with some of the legislation they've  passed to try and require companies to  house and have offices there and those  are ways of trying to exert pressure  on on companies so it's it's a little  bit  hard to say that they're in collusion  with governments they're in a space  where they're trying to preserve their  own best interests and we should never  lose sight of that that as much as we  rely on them sometimes to help us  protect rights they also have their own  their own interests and their own  threats and that will not necessarily  always be in line with the protection of  human rights uh melody part three let's  just leave alone the corporation uh and  the idea of social media giants just for  a second this also comes down to  individuals as well i'll give you a  personal case uh whenever i've flown to  the us certainly in the last couple of  years um well actually before the  pandemic now actually i'm losing track  of time here um i've often been asked to  give my social media passwords  as an individual going through secondary  immigration  there's been no compunction to actually  give those over but the tone is if you  don't it's going to be very difficult  for you to enter so governments are  using this on an individual level is  that how alarming  is that for you  it is very concerning and if we put this  in the context of increased surveillance  online and also the targeting the  illegal targeting of journalists human  rights defenders  human rights lawyers across the world i  mean in the past few months there have  been a number of revelations about such  surveillance practice  so it is alarming indeed because we are  seeing that governments themselves have  their own agenda and that their own  agenda sometimes are not legitimate um  as much as yes this information is an  issue it has to be tackled because it is  a threat to our democracies likewise  incitement to violence online  sexist uh abused racist abuse and so on  like there are issues that need to be  addressed actually they need to be  addressed offline as well as online we  are also seeing um  a trend towards control towards  surveillance towards an erosion of our  civic space and towards an erosion of  our rights and of our democratic values  and some some of the some of the that  threat has come directly from some  governments and sometimes even from some  governments  that are not so-called authoritarian or  that can push forward some um  well-intentioned measures so this is  something that we cannot deny  as much as  we we are facing lots of different  challenges and we need to address and i  think in this community experts are  still  finding like discussing to find  solutions appropriate solutions to these  issues but we also must recognize that  attacks against the media attacks  against the press surveillance online  targeting of human rights defenders and  an attempt to  censor  the internet has has become a biggest  issue in the past few years vic lenza  but this is not just about the social  media giants it's not just about  governance here because often google  facebook um instagram all of their ceos  will turn around and say actually we are  trying to protect people's freedom of  speeches leave it to us we are best  placed to be able to do this but isn't  that a bit like putting the fox in  charge of the hen house  yeah so i actually argued exactly what  you're just saying in a sense in  particular for facebook because along  for the longest time it was  self-regulation is the best kind of  regulation and obviously even facebook  facebook's effort for example with its  so-called oversight board which tries to  be diverse and sort of have these very  in a way esteemed individuals who uh are  members of that board  the thing is that it i just want to  correct something in the sense that uh  there are some conflicting interests  that they have themselves uh so what  these corporations in the end they  depend on the trust of their users they  depend on their users liking their  products and and sort of being able to  um so in that sense if people  continuously feel attacked and safe or  if it ends up being something that they  don't want to use there's obviously  their disincentive on their side and not  an interest to actually have so there is  an interest on their in their side to  sort of say hey we rather have this  blamed on the regulator the fact that we  have to shut down your account rather  than say oh you have to hate us for  doing that or this is something that  we've decided in a way it also provides  a cover for them so to speak that you  can sort of  blame it or so to speak say that it's a  question of compliance rather than  saying it's a question of them deciding  something because in the end it gives  them that buffer that they can host a  variety of individuals some more freedom  of speech oriented others may be more  privacy oriented human rights activists  or whatnot the challenge overall here in  terms of the regulation who regulates  what and and how and how that should be  done is that there is a question  generally here of as i said also before  there is the stake of just the kind of  power that they have and in the end of  the day uh a corporation can no longer  just simply hide behind this corporate  veil and say we're only accountable to  our shareholders when they're actually  holding so much public power and so much  public influence globally whether it's  elections whether it's speech whether  it's protests  and so in that sense uh it's it's not  sufficient to sort of say that we're  able to initially to to limit our own  powers  but i have to say that there has been a  shift in that direction from a lot of  companies and what's disappointing  i'm really sorry we are running out of  time but there is a question i want to  ask all three of you just very quickly  i'll begin with uh quinn mchugh i've had  a social media account for over i think  12 13 years now why is legislation taken  so long  or even the thought and the talk of  legislation  it's an interesting question i think  the  initially early on the global approach  to these companies i think had was led  by  um some of the way they were treated in  the united states which is basically  hands off we want this new technology  this new way of communicating to  flourish we want to treat these  companies as just merely intermediaries  um for the free flow of information and  therefore we want to protect them as  neutral intermediaries right from  adverse actions because of the content  which would be on them which is  inherently speech restricting or rights  violating to punish the intermediary for  the the content that's hosted sorry we  are running out of time i do want to  come to the other guests as well vig  lenses just very quickly just uh very  quickly what are your thoughts  my quick tweet if i may say so to your  question would be that uh it's actually  not true that we're just talking about  it now but i think we're just talking  with the level of alarm and urgency and  widespread globally more so because i  think many of us have actually expressed  concerns but of course i agree with the  previous speaker that we've also  expressed an excitement but i think it's  a fantastic opportune moment to have  more public awareness and to hopefully  have better tools going forward both  from the legislators and public pressure  to end up having better rules than wha